Chapter: TreeSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Tree
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Tree: the screen layout of a conifer, its eight crown tiers, and the convex hull
 that turns a projected ring into a cone's silhouette.

 THE HULL IS THE REASON THIS SPEC EXISTS. Everything else here is a table or a
 product; `convex-hull-pts` is Andrew's monotone chain, it decides the POINT
 COUNT of every near tier, and the judge compares that count exactly. It is also
 the one thing in the chapter with genuine edge cases, and they are graded from
 hand-made integer points rather than from a projected ring -- a ring is sixteen
 points in general position, which exercises none of them.

 FOUR CASES, AND EACH ONE IS A DIFFERENT DECISION:
   * an interior point is dropped, and so is a COLLINEAR one -- the trim test is
     `<= 0`, which is the zig's choice, and swapping it for `< 0` keeps collinear
     points and changes the count without changing the picture;
   * three points in general position come back as three;
   * four points all on one line come back as TWO, not four and not zero;
   * fewer than three points come back UNSORTED and untouched, because the
     function short-circuits before it sorts -- so a caller handing in two gets
     them in the order it gave them.

 The last is worth stating because it is the one an implementation "tidies up" by
 sorting first and returning early after: that version answers the same set and a
 different list.

 THE METRICS ARE CHOSEN TO LAND ON WHOLE PIXELS. A tree 4 metres tall at 2 metres
 forward through a focal of 500 gives a pixel height of exactly 1000, and every
 fraction of it below -- 0.648 foliage, 0.44 trunk, 0.288 half-width -- is then a
 whole number too.

 We say:

Section: The Proportions

 Eight tiers, narrow at the top and widest at the bottom, each a fraction of the
 foliage span. The three tables are read in step, so their lengths are part of
 the contract.

  table-got : List Real
  table-got = tier-top & tier-bot & tier-wide

  table-want : List Real
  table-want =
    [ 0.0, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70,
      0.30, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.70, 0.80, 0.90, 1.0,
      0.35, 0.44, 0.53, 0.63, 0.72, 0.81, 0.91, 1.0 ]

  size-got : List Integer
  size-got = [list-length tier-top, list-length tier-bot, list-length tier-wide, ring-n, trunk-color]

  size-want : List Integer
  size-want = [8, 8, 8, 16, 5914146]

  frac-got : List Real
  frac-got = [visible-trunk, crown-h, crown-w, min-cone-forward]

  frac-want : List Real
  frac-want = [0.44, 0.648, 0.288, 0.4]

Section: The Layout

 The pixel height is the gap between the projected feet and the projected top, so
 perspective is applied once to a scalar. The crown hangs below the apex and the
 trunk shows for the fraction under it.

  m : Metrics
  m = metrics 0.0 2.0 4.0 500.0 960.0

  metric-got : List Real
  metric-got = [m.bx, m.by, m.ht, m.foliage, m.apex-y, m.w]

  metric-want : List Real
  metric-want = [480.0, 600.0, 1000.0, 648.0, -488.0, 288.0]

 The trunk is eight per cent of the height wide with a one-pixel floor, and it
 runs from the ground up past the crown's base by a twentieth.

  trunk : List DrawCmd
  trunk = draw-trunk m False

  trunk-got : List Real
  trunk-got = (list-at trunk 0).pts

  trunk-want : List Real
  trunk-want = [440.0, 110.0, 520.0, 110.0, 520.0, 600.0, 440.0, 600.0]

 Round or flat is the caller's choice and it is the only thing that changes: same
 four points, a different tag, and the round one carries full strength.

  trunk-tag-got : List Integer
  trunk-tag-got =
    let r = draw-trunk m True
    in [ (list-at trunk 0).tag, (list-at r 0).tag,
         (list-at trunk 0).color, (list-at r 0).color,
         list-length (list-at r 0).pts ]

  trunk-tag-want : List Integer
  trunk-tag-want = [0, 1, 5914146, 5914146, 8]

  trunk-strength-got : List Real
  trunk-strength-got = [(list-at trunk 0).strength, (list-at (draw-trunk m True) 0).strength]

  trunk-strength-want : List Real
  trunk-strength-want = [0.0, 1.0]

 A flat tier is an isosceles triangle: apex on the trunk axis, two feet either
 side at the tier's own half-width. The top tier's feet land on the crown base,
 which is where the trunk takes over.

  tri-got : List Real
  tri-got = (list-at (tier-triangle m 0 7) 0).pts & (list-at (tier-triangle m 7 7) 0).pts

  tri-want : List Real
  tri-want =
    [ 480.0, -488.0, 580.8, -293.6, 379.2, -293.6,
      480.0, -34.4, 768.0, 160.0, 192.0, 160.0 ]

Section: The Hull

 Hand-made points, so every cross product is an integer and the turn direction is
 not a rounding question.

  p : Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  p (x) (y) = ScreenPt { x = x, y = y }

  xy : List ScreenPt -> List Real
  xy (ps) = flatten-screen ps 0

 A square with a point inside it and a point in the middle of its bottom edge.
 Both are dropped, and the four corners come back in hull order.

  square : List ScreenPt
  square = [p 0.0 0.0, p 4.0 0.0, p 4.0 4.0, p 0.0 4.0, p 2.0 2.0, p 2.0 0.0]

  square-got : List Real
  square-got = xy (convex-hull-pts square)

  square-want : List Real
  square-want = [0.0, 0.0, 4.0, 0.0, 4.0, 4.0, 0.0, 4.0]

 Three points in general position are their own hull, and the sort is what puts
 them in this order rather than the order they were written.

  tri3-got : List Real
  tri3-got = xy (convex-hull-pts [p 0.0 0.0, p 4.0 0.0, p 2.0 3.0])

  tri3-want : List Real
  tri3-want = [0.0, 0.0, 4.0, 0.0, 2.0, 3.0]

 Four points on one line collapse to the two ends -- the case where dropping
 collinear points is the whole answer rather than a detail of it.

  line-got : List Real
  line-got = xy (convex-hull-pts [p 0.0 0.0, p 1.0 1.0, p 2.0 2.0, p 3.0 3.0])

  line-want : List Real
  line-want = [0.0, 0.0, 3.0, 3.0]

 Under three points, the function returns its argument untouched and UNSORTED.

  short-got : List Real
  short-got = xy (convex-hull-pts [p 1.0 2.0, p 0.0 0.0]) & xy (convex-hull-pts [p 9.0 9.0]) & xy (convex-hull-pts [])

  short-want : List Real
  short-want = [1.0, 2.0, 0.0, 0.0, 9.0, 9.0]

 A repeated corner does not become an extra hull vertex.

  dup-got : List Real
  dup-got = xy (convex-hull-pts [p 0.0 0.0, p 0.0 0.0, p 4.0 0.0, p 4.0 4.0])

  dup-want : List Real
  dup-want = [0.0, 0.0, 4.0, 0.0, 4.0, 4.0]

  count-got : List Integer
  count-got =
    [ list-length (convex-hull-pts square),
      list-length (convex-hull-pts [p 0.0 0.0, p 4.0 0.0, p 2.0 3.0]),
      list-length (convex-hull-pts [p 0.0 0.0, p 1.0 1.0, p 2.0 2.0, p 3.0 3.0]),
      list-length (convex-hull-pts [p 1.0 2.0, p 0.0 0.0]),
      list-length (convex-hull-pts [p 0.0 0.0, p 0.0 0.0, p 4.0 0.0, p 4.0 4.0]) ]

  count-want : List Integer
  count-want = [4, 3, 2, 2, 3]

 The sort orders by x and breaks ties by y, which is what makes the two chains
 monotone. Grading it directly is what says a hull failure is the chains and not
 the order they were handed.

  sorted-got : List Real
  sorted-got = xy (sort-pts square 0 [])

  sorted-want : List Real
  sorted-want = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 4.0, 2.0, 0.0, 2.0, 2.0, 4.0, 0.0, 4.0, 4.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-table " table-got table-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-size  " size-got size-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-frac  " frac-got frac-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-metric" metric-got metric-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-trunk " trunk-got trunk-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-ttag  " trunk-tag-got trunk-tag-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-tstr  " trunk-strength-got trunk-strength-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-tri   " tri-got tri-want 0.000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-square" square-got square-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-tri3  " tri3-got tri3-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-line  " line-got line-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-short " short-got short-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-dup   " dup-got dup-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "tr-count " count-got count-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "tr-sorted" sorted-got sorted-want 0.0)
  end
